WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.259
OK, so when do you play Monopoly?

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:11.969
I have not played Monopoly since I was, like,
ten.

00:00:11.969 --> 00:00:12.969
So…

00:00:12.969 --> 00:00:19.560
Now I play it when I’m at my parents’
house, if there’s a big family gathering.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.490
I don’t ever play Monopoly.

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:24.949
Basically, you played Monopoly when you were
a kid or when you were stuck inside.

00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:31.390
So, I talked to somebody who played Monopoly
in a ballroom in the Venetian hotel in Macau.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:32.810
I’m Brian Valentine.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:37.980
I was the United States representative to
the Monopoly World Championships in 2015,

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.070
where I finished third amongst the 28 competitors.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:46.219
He taught me all the right ways to play Monopoly
— and even make the game a little shorter.

00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:51.489
All the rules, odds, and strategies that can
actually help you win.

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:56.649
This is a Monopoly board from...

00:00:56.649 --> 00:01:03.769
..Target...but I took it with me to Macau
and asked all the players to sign it for me.

00:01:03.769 --> 00:01:05.350
Just as a keepsake.

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:13.460
I’m almost getting into sports cliches talking
about Monopoly, but tt was very humbling to

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:19.000
have made it that far against players who
are the best in their country and who are

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.299
fantastic people.

00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:24.680
And this Monopoly genius, he knows heatmaps
and housing arbitrage and strategy — but

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:27.240
he just wants you to follow the rules.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.990
OK, what happens on free parking?

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.780
Do you, like, collect money from people?

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:36.130
There’s no money under free parking, I guess
that’s the biggest.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:40.150
You don’t put money under free parking,
people see as a chance to equalize the game

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:41.180
that’s stacked against them.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:44.399
If you keep putting money in the game, it
stops the progress.

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:46.110
People don’t play the game by the rules.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:49.049
If they did, it wouldn’t take quite so long.

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:50.560
Do you auction stuff off?

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:51.560
No.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.479
I remember reading it on the back of the card,
but I don’t remember — I remember that

00:01:55.479 --> 00:01:58.770
always feeling a little over my head in terms
of play.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:03.340
If you land on it and don’t want it, it’s
got to go up for auction on the spot.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:04.340
Somebody can buy it.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:05.530
You can start the bidding at a dollar.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:09.800
I’ve seen it happen, and I’ve seen someone
take Boardwalk for 20 bucks.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:14.569
Once you have the rules in order, you’ve
gotta go with the strategy.

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:16.620
What’s the best spot in Monopoly?

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:18.530
Oh, I’m sure it’s like Boardwalk.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:22.280
It’s funny because that’s the glamour,
that’s the trademark of Monopoly, it’s

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:23.280
Boardwalk - you gotta get it.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.069
The object of the game is to take everyone’s
money and be the only person left in the game.

00:02:27.069 --> 00:02:31.849
While Boardwalk certainly offers the prospect
of that — $2000 when you get a hotel on

00:02:31.849 --> 00:02:33.920
it — think about what it costs to get there.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.640
You’ve got to buy each of the two properties.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.200
Then it’s 200 dollars, per house per property,
until you get to that level.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:49.020
So that’s another thousand dollars on each
one to then get $2000 back.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:51.080
And there are only two of them, right?

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.909
Most of the other Monopolies have three spaces
that you can hit and then draw rent.

00:02:54.909 --> 00:02:58.959
If you are building up Boardwalk and Park
Place before anybody else has built Monopolies

00:02:58.959 --> 00:03:02.170
up against you, hell yeah, go for it.

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:06.600
But if you’re in a situation where other
folks have Monopolies on that hot side of

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.980
the board, and you’re waiting to throw the
haymaker, if you will, on Boardwalk and Park

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:16.790
Place, that’s a loser more than it’s a
winner.

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:26.000
You can go on any website and learn the basic
parlor trick that the oranges are the best

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.220
Monopoly to have.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:33.959
Are you gonna now show a chart of the Monopoly
board with a heat map?

00:03:33.959 --> 00:03:36.849
I’m sad to say that I will.

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:39.489
I knew that existed.

00:03:39.489 --> 00:03:44.500
The reason that that is so is because jail
is one of the most often landed on spaces

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.060
in the game.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:54.930
The fact that the common role is 7, then 7
from jail — while not an actual property,

00:03:54.930 --> 00:04:00.450
it’s Community Chest — 6 and 8, the next
most likely numbers, are St. James and Tennessee

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:01.450
Avenue.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:05.170
So you know that the oranges are likely to
get hit on.

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:11.010
And also, by extension, the section between
jail and go to jail that side’s the side

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:12.060
you want to be building on.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:14.830
It’s feast or famine on the other side of
the board.

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:19.190
So there’s a couple more strategies that
are on the practical, not so mathy end.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:23.330
If you get to jail early, go ahead pay up,
get out of there.

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:26.650
You can also use your houses as weapons to
control the game.

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:28.630
You have 32 houses in the game.

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:32.090
You’ve got to build houses before you can
build hotels.

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:38.230
The houses have to be physically present to
be bought, which is why, in a lot of circles

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:41.190
you’ll hear people say about creating a
housing shortage.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:45.430
You want to get to the four house level so
you can pull the trigger and get hotels if

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:50.550
you need to, or you can sit on the four houses
and keep other people from having them.

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:52.300
OK, so final question.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:05:03.250
So when I say what is the right way to play
Monopoly, how do you respond?

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:07.450
The right way to play Monopoly is just...don’t
play it.

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:11.430
Don’t steal from the bank.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:16.720
And that goes out to my cousins when we played
when we were ten.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.120
I would say, is it just like, to win?

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:22.700
How to win?

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:25.080
With honor.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:34.500
It’s almost, sometimes, you feel like you
have this Renaissance era sense of honor and

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:35.500
dignity.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.960
Not that we’re going around slapping each
other with gloves having duels or anything

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:39.130
like that.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:42.990
But...that’s the thing that keeps coming
to me is honor.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:50.750
Playing Monopoly allows you to get to know
people in a way that formal introductions

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:55.910
or being coworkers, or whatever, doesn’t
quite show.

00:05:55.910 --> 00:06:01.910
Because in an hour and half, two hour game,
you’re gonna see how people handle adversity,

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:08.770
you’re gonna see how people handle success,
you’re gonna see, bundled in this short

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:17.120
format, that allows you to see who a person
is.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:34.470
Ahhh...shhh….it’s Monopoly, we’re talking
about Monopoly.

